# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this repository is LF, in the index and on disk, on every
# platform. Before this file existed that was only a convention, and the
# convention had already lost: 69 tracked files were CRLF, 12 were mixed, and
# three source files carried a byte that made git classify them as binary. A
# scripted edit that met one of those files quietly rewrote all of it, which
# buries a two-line change in a whole-file diff.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto` on purpose: `text=auto` alone leaves the
# working tree to `core.autocrlf`, which is a per-machine setting, so two
# developers would still disagree about what is on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Upstream Angband's own bytes, at the 4.2.6 tag. A few of these ship CRLF from
# upstream (Makefile.nmake, docs/make.bat, three Shockbolt/Nomad .prf files) and
# normalising them would make the parity baseline differ from the baseline.
reference/** -text

# Windows batch files are only reliably CRLF, so any that are ever added outside
# reference/ keep it.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Detected correctly by `text=auto` anyway, but stated so that a truncated or
# unusual header can never make one of them look like text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.bmp binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.node binary
*.pdf binary

# LAST MATCH WINS, and that is the whole reason this line is repeated here.
#
# The `reference/** -text` rule above was defeated by `*.bat text eol=crlf`
# three lines below it: a bare `*.bat` pattern matches at ANY depth, so it
# re-applied `eol=crlf` to `reference/docs/make.bat` and put that file back
# under normalisation. `git check-attr -a` said `text: set`, and the comment on
# the `*.bat` rule ("any that are ever added outside reference/") described an
# intent the pattern did not express.
#
# The symptom was remote and looked unrelated: on CI's Linux checkout the file
# came out differing from the index, and `reference-tree.test.ts` - the guard
# that proves reference/ is still blob-identical to the 4.2.6 tag - failed with
# "reference/ differs between the working tree and the index". Every local gate
# was green, because on Windows the conversion is a no-op.
#
# Repeating the exemption last is deliberate rather than tidy: any future rule
# added above this line cannot silently start rewriting upstream's bytes.
reference/** -text
